{
  "id": "urn:visionflow:page:9ef7ee9235c35c0c898f46e5fc48ecb532278239bba6dc548df9b497c84ad72c",
  "title": "Bitcoin Environmental Issues",
  "slug": "bitcoin-environmental-issues",
  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:visionflow:owl:class:bitcoin-environmental-issues",
  "domain": "blockchain",
  "definition": "**Bitcoin Environmental Issues** refers to the multi-dimensional ecological footprint generated by [[Bitcoin]]'s [[Proof of Work]] (PoW) consensus mechanism and the extensive [[ASIC]] mining hardware infrastructure that sustains it. The concept encompasses electricity consumption measured and tracked by the [[Cambridge Bitcoin Electricity Consumption Index]] ([[CBECI]]), greenhouse gas emissions expressed as CO\u2082 equivalent (CO\u2082e), electronic waste (e-waste) from specialised hardware with accelerated obsolescence cycles, water consumption in cooling systems, land use, and noise pollution. Counterbalancing arguments and empirical evidence document that Bitcoin mining can serve as a flexible demand sink for [[Stranded Energy]], a catalyst for [[Renewable Energy]] project development, a monetisation pathway for otherwise-vented [[Methane Emissions]] from fossil-fuel extraction sites, and a source of recoverable waste heat for district heating and agricultural applications.",
